WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:02.659
Film Courage: Why do most people fail at screenwriting?

00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:09.180
John Truby: I think most people fail at screenwriting
because it’s the most difficult craft in

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.180
the world.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.019
You know, people say talk about “I want
to be a director.”

00:00:13.019 --> 00:00:18.450
Well, being a director is a very difficult
challenging job.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.400
But it doesn’t compare to writing.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.310
The craft of storytelling is the most complex
craft in the world.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:30.080
It takes a lifetime commitment to master.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:36.220
Now a lot of writers either don't realize
it’s going to take a lifetime of commitment

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.530
or they don’t want to face a lifetime of
commitment.

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:43.660
It’s a lot of work and that’s a lot of
time.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:48.620
Most writers are looking for the magic bullet.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.660
Which is why I talk about this in my class
all the time.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:58.150
Most writers start off with this three act
structure-way of looking at storytelling,

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:03.250
which is designed for beginners and does not
work at the professional level.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:07.720
The reason it’s so popular is because it
is a kind of magic bullet.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.390
“It’s not that hard!”

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:17.680
Well, we’d all like to think that writing
a successful screenplay can be done with a

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.740
few simple steps, you know, a paint-by-numbers
approach.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.170
Unfortunately it doesn’t work.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.420
You have to be able to put in the work.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:35.960
And you have to also realize that the level
of training that is required to get to a level

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.600
of excellence.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:45.590
And that means literally hundred of craft
techniques that have to be mastered.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:53.100
I will always tell writers in my class, plot
is the most underestimated of all the major

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.119
writing skills.

00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:59.770
Most writers know the importance of a good
main character, they know the importance of

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.159
lean dialogue and so on.

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:05.200
But when it comes to plot, they think well
I’ll just figure that out as I go.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.429
And of course that never happens because it
turns out that plot has more techniques that

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:19.349
must be mastered to become a working professional
than all the other major writing skills combined.

00:02:19.349 --> 00:02:25.010
If you don’t know what those techniques
are, you have really no chance of working

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:26.849
at that level.

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:32.150
So that is why I say it is so important to
writers to not just getting trained, but getting

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:38.489
the right kind of training so that they are
working at the professional level.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:47.809
And one of the key hallmarks that I’ve tried
to teach in all of my classes is that professional

00:02:47.809 --> 00:02:52.579
writers use fundamentally different techniques
than all other writers.

00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:57.450
If you don’t know what those professional
techniques are, you cannot play in that game,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.319
you cannot compete at that level.

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:04.040
Film Courage: Do you think too that a lot
of people don’t realize that their temperament

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.409
is not suited [for being a writer].

00:03:05.409 --> 00:03:06.930
I mean they are spending a lot of time alone.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.790
You are having to be sort of self-generating.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:13.499
And maybe people don’t realize how difficult
that is?

00:03:13.499 --> 00:03:14.730
John Truby: You are absolutely right.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:21.069
I consider the psychological element the biggest
obstacle to a writer’s success.

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:28.670
Because the difficulty of what you just said,
the difficulty of facing the page by yourself,

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:38.980
being in that room alone, trying to meet that
challenge, day-in-and-day-out, is very difficult

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:41.749
on the human mind.

00:03:41.749 --> 00:03:49.969
And writers first of all don’t realize what
it’s going to take psychologically at first.

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:55.260
And then when they get into it and they try
to deal with that.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.799
They really don’t have the tools or the
support system to be able to be in it for

00:03:59.799 --> 00:04:00.799
the long haul.

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:08.129
I often tell writers that people who are professional
are the people who are still standing, you

00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:09.129
know?

00:04:09.129 --> 00:04:14.870
Everybody else…they were knocked out…for
various reasons, like they did not get the

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:15.870
right training.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:21.630
But most of all they cannot handle the psychological
difficulties of what it takes to write at

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.450
the professional level.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:31.310
Meaning, writing alone, facing rejection,
again and again, over many years and so on

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:36.890
until you have mastered the craft at a level
that somebody says “Hey, you’re a good

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:37.890
writer.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.110
This is a good script.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.900
I’m going to hire you.”

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:46.370
So it’s the people who A) commit to the
craft and B)

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:53.550
who are still standing who are there after
many years so that finally somebody says “Yeah,

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.350
you’ve done the work.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.430
You’re actually pretty good.”

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:03.030
Because keep in mind, most people don’t
know a good story.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.500
The just know [either] I liked it or I didn’t
like it.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:08.060
But they are not trained as writers.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:11.670
Again it’s the most complex craft in the
world.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:17.850
So part of the difficult psychology is how
do you get over the fact that you’re getting

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.360
rejected from people who don’t know what
you do.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.850
That’s very tough to face.

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:29.300
Film Courage: So which do you think is more
important, stubbornness or a teflon personality

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:36.180
where rejection just doesn’t define who
you are?

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.590
John Truby: I would say neither of those.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:45.580
I would say both of those are dependent on
a previous requirement which is maintaining

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:50.810
an openness to learning the craft.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:59.430
I will often get professional writers to take
my class and when I talk to them what I always

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:03.140
find is these people have no ego.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:08.220
At least no ego coming across that I can see.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:15.960
Because they are professional writers so you
would think that they think “Well I already

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:16.960
know this stuff!”

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:17.960
But they don’t.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:18.960
They don’t take that attitude.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:19.960
Yes, they are extremely well-trained.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:25.030
If they get to the level of a professional
writer, they are extremely well-trained.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:30.270
But these are people who are open to learning
more.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:38.570
And I find that is the most important quality
for being a successful writer.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:48.671
The willingness to learn from anywhere, anyone,
so that your repertoire, your arsenal, your

00:06:48.671 --> 00:06:53.180
set of tools is constantly growing.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:59.930
That (in turn) gives you the confidence to
say “That person didn't like my script,

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:07.350
but I know that it is based on the craft elements
that make for a good story.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:11.520
So whether they liked it or not, that doesn’t
effect me.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.360
I know it’s a good story.”

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.210
That’s where you get that foundation of
confidence that you need to get through those

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:18.389
tough psychological moments.

00:07:18.389 --> 00:07:24.800
Film Courage: So almost as if, [let’s say]
an attorney or a doctor sort of mandatory

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:30.020
continuing education, artists some how feel
they are immune to this [same kind of training]?

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:31.130
“I’ve already learned what I need to learn…”

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:32.130
John Truby: Most do.

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:33.130
Most do.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:34.800
And it’s a huge problem.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:46.000
You know, when I give classes the most common
question is all about “How do I sell my

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.000
script?”

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:52.250
There’s an implication in that question
which is “I already know how to write a

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:53.260
good script.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:59.360
The only thing I’m not able to do is sell
it.”

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:00.360
Not so.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:03.960
99 percent of writers, will always talk in
Hollywood about, “It’s all about the connections.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.400
It’s all about who you know.”

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:13.490
That is so overrated. 99 percent of writers
when they finally meet someone who is a useful

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:18.330
connection, they don’t have the story tools
to make that connection pay off.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:22.940
They don’t have the craft foundation.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:32.140
So it’s all about this ability to treat
writing as a profession.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:40.019
And part of that is “I can never know enough
about how this craft works.”

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:43.889
And partly because every story is different.

00:08:43.889 --> 00:08:49.170
I’ve talked to professional writer friends
who…we’re always amazed.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:52.029
We think it’s going to get easier now.

00:08:52.029 --> 00:08:53.589
It doesn’t!

00:08:53.589 --> 00:08:55.620
It gets harder.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:04.980
Because the better you get, the higher you
set the bar for yourself on your script.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:10.579
Cracking a story is always hard no matter
how much you know because every story is totally

00:09:10.579 --> 00:09:11.990
unique.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:17.350
Yes there are certain elements you can use
that you can hang your hat on that you can

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:19.750
get a way into the story and so on.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.820
But every one is tough.

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:29.839
So that’s why having this professional approach
to being a writer or being a storyteller is

00:09:29.839 --> 00:09:34.240
so important because A) it makes you successful
B)

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.389
it gets you through.

